Solved with **dolphinday**. Kinda wonky problem but neat observation.

\[\left(\frac{a}{b+c}+\frac{b}{c+a}+\frac{c}{a+b}\right)(a+b+c) = \frac{a^2}{b+c}+\frac{b^2}{c+a}+\frac{c^2}{a+b}+(a+b+c)\]
Thus, 
\[9(a+b+c)=32+(a+b+c)\]
From this, we conclude that  $a+b+c=4$ . Further, note that, 
\[\left(\frac{a^2}{b+c}+\frac{b^2}{c+a}+\frac{c^2}{a+b}\right)(a+b+c) = \frac{a^3}{b+c}+\frac{b^3}{c+a}+\frac{c^3}{a+b}+(a^2+b^2+c^2)\]
Thus, 
\[(a^2+b^2+c^2)+122 = 32 \cdot 4\]
From this we conclude that  $a^2+b^2+c^2=6$ . From this note that since  $(a+b+c)^2=a^2+b^2+c^2+2(ab+bc+ca)$  we have that  $ab+bc+ca=5$  as well. Plugging all this information into the first equation we have, 
\begin{align*}
    9 &= \frac{a}{b+c}+\frac{b}{c+a}+\frac{c}{a+b}
    &= \frac{a}{4-a}+\frac{b}{4-b}+\frac{c}{4-c}
    &= \frac{16(a+b+c)-8(ab+bc+ca)+3abc}{64-16(a+b+c)+4(ab+bc+ca)-abc}
    9 &= \frac{24+3abc}{20-abc}
    180-9abc&= 24+3abc
    12abc&=156
\end{align*}
From which we can conclude that  $abc = \boxed{13}$  which is the requested answer.

