solution_md:
Cute generating functions problem.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>First, note a number is divisible by  $11$  if and only if its alternating digit sums have a difference that is a multiple of  $11.$ It is not difficult to see that the generating function that encodes the difference between the alternating digit sums of a nonnegative integer with at most  $40$  digits consisting of entirely zeros and ones is
\begin{align*}
F(x) = (1+x)^{20}(1+x^{-1})^{20}.
\end{align*}
Now, let  $[x^n] F(x)$  denote the coefficient of  $x^n$  in some generating function  $F(x)$  where  $n$  is a constant and let us define  $\{x^n\} F(x)$  for some constant  $n$  and generating function  $F(x)$  as
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty} [x^{kn}].
\end{align*}
It follows that we wish to find
\begin{align*}
\{x^{11}\} F(x) &= \{x^{11}\} (1+x)^{20}(1+x^{-1})^{20} 
&= \{x^{11}\} (1+x)^{20} (1+x)^{20} \cdot x^{-20} 
&= \{x^{11}\} (1+x)^{40} \cdot x^2.
\end{align*}
By the Binomial Theorem, this is just
\begin{align*}
[x^{11}] x^2(1+x)^{40} + [x^{22}] x^2(1+x)^{40}+[x^{33}] x^2(1+x)^{40} &= [x^9] (1+x)^{40} + [x^{20}] (1+x)^{40} + [x^{31}] (1+x)^{40} 
&= \binom{40}{9} + \binom{40}{20} + \binom{40}{31} 
&= \boxed{2 \cdot \binom{40}{9} + \binom{40}{20}}.
\end{align*} $\square$</details>

