We have
\[\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{\phi(n)}{5^n+1}=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\phi(n)\sum_{m=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^{m+1}}{5^{mn}}=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{5^n}\sum_{d\mid n}\phi(d)(-1)^{\frac{n}{d}+1}.\]
If  $n$  is odd, then
\[\sum_{d\mid n}\phi(d)(-1)^{\frac{n}{d}+1}=\sum_{d\mid n}\phi(d)=n.\]
If  $n=2^kq$ , where  $q$  is odd and  $k\geq 1$ , then
\[\sum_{d\mid n}\phi(d)(-1)^{\frac{n}{d}+1}=\sum_{\text{odd }d\mid n}(-\phi(d)-\phi(2d)-\dotsb+\phi(2^kd))=0.\]
(The second sum is over all odd divisors of  $n$ .) Hence
\[\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{\phi(n)}{5^n+1}=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{2k+1}{5^{2k+1}}=\boxed{\frac{65}{288}}.\]
<details><summary>details for the last computation</summary>We have
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{5}+\frac{1}{5^3}+\frac{1}{5^5}+\frac{1}{5^7}+\dotsb &= \frac{5}{24} 
\frac{2}{5^3}+\frac{2}{5^5}+\frac{2}{5^7}+\dotsb &= \frac{1}{60} 
\frac{2}{5^5}+\frac{2}{5^7}+\dotsb &= \frac{1}{25\times 60} 
\frac{2}{5^7}+\dotsb &= \frac{1}{25^2\times 60} 
&\vdots
\end{align*}
Add up all of these to obtain
\[\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{2k+1}{5^{2k+1}}=\frac{5}{24}+\frac{1}{60}\left(1+\frac{1}{25}+\frac{1}{25^2}+\dotsb\right)=\frac{5}{24}+\frac{1}{60}\times\frac{25}{24}=\frac{65}{288}.\]</details>

