Let  $O$  be the circumcenter of  $\triangle ABC$ .**<span style="color:#f00">Claim.**</span>  $B, M, O, N, C, P$  are concyclic.

*Proof.* Verify that  $\angle OMC = \frac 12 AMC = \angle OBC$ , and similarly for  $N$ , so  $M, N, P$  all lie on  $(BOC)$  as required.  $\blacksquare$ Now, let  $R$  be the radius of  $(BOC)$ . We may write 
\begin{align*}
MN&= 2R\sin A = BP=CP = 5 
PM &= 2R\sin C 
PN &= 2R \sin B 
BC &= 2R \sin(2A).
\end{align*}
To extract  $BM$  and  $Cn$ , notice that the circumradius  $OB = 2R \cos A$ , so 
\begin{align*}
AB &= 2(2R\cos A) \sin C 
AC &= 2(2R \cos A) \sin B 
\end{align*}
so   $$ BM = 4R\left(\cos A \sin C - \frac 12 \sin B\right). $$   Summing cyclically,   $$ BM+CN = 4R(\cos A \sin C + \cos A \sin B- \frac 12 \sin B - \frac 12 \sin C) = 4R(\sin B + \sin C)\left(\cos A - \frac 12 \right). $$   Now, the two given conditions yield 
\begin{align*}
PM+PN= 16 &\implies R(\sin B + \sin C) = 8 
BC+BM+CN = 24 &\implies BM+CN = 24-2R \sin(2A).
\end{align*}
Then   $$ \frac{BM+CN}8 = \frac{BM+CN}{PM+PN} = 4\left(\cos A - \frac 12\right), $$   so 
\begin{align*}
12-R\sin(2A) &= 16\left(\cos A - \frac 12\right) 
12-2R \sin A \cos A &= 16\cos A - 8 
12-5 \cos A &= 16 \cos A - 8,
\end{align*}
thus  $\cos A = \frac{10}{21}$ . Then   $$ BC=2(2R \sin A)(\cos A) = 10 \cdot \frac{20}{21} = \boxed{\frac{200}{21}}. $$

