<details><summary>Solution</summary>From  $\triangle{AMC}$  and  $\triangle{ANB}$  being isosceles, we have  $\angle{BMC}=2A=\angle{BNC},$  so quadrilateral  $BMNC$  is cyclic. Since  $PB$  and  $PC$  are tangent to the circumcircle,  $\angle{BMC}+\angle{BPC}=2A+180^{\circ}-2A=180^{\circ}$ , so points  $B, M, N, C, P$  are concyclic. Thus,  $\angle{BMP}=\angle{BCP}=\angle{BAC}$ , meaning  $MP\parallel{AC}$ . Similarly,  $NP\parallel{AB}$ . In particular,  $AMPN$  is a parallelogram, and  $\triangle{AMN}\cong\triangle{PNM}$ . 
[asy]
import olympiad;
size(200);
defaultpen(linewidth(0.5)+fontsize(10));
pair A=dir(105);
pair B=dir(230); 
pair C=dir(310);
pair O=circumcenter(A,B,C);
pair M=intersectionpoint(circumcircle(O,B,C),A--B);
pair N=intersectionpoint(circumcircle(O,B,C),A--C);
pair P=rotate(180,(M+N)/2)*A;
draw(B--C--N--M--cycle, deepgreen);
draw(P--B, deepgreen); draw(P--C, deepgreen);
draw(A--M--P--N--cycle, red);
draw(circumcircle(A,B,C), royalblue);
draw(circumcircle(O,B,C), orange+dashed);
dot(" $A$ ",A,dir(105));
dot(" $B$ ",B,dir(230));
dot(" $C$ ",C,dir(310));
dot(" $M$ ",M,dir(150));
dot(" $N$ ",N,dir(30));
dot(" $P$ ",P,dir(270));
[/asy]
As  $BMNP$  is a cyclic trapezoid,  $PB=MN=5$ . From the given perimeters,  $AM+AN=16$ , and   $$ BM+CN+BC=AB+AC-(AM+AN)+BC=AB+AC+BC-16=24\implies{AB+AC+BC=40}. $$   Observe that  $\triangle{ANM}\sim\triangle{ABC}$ , so   $$ \frac{BC}{5}=\frac{40}{21}\implies{BC}=\boxed{\frac{200}{21}} $$</details>

