Let us substitute  $2023$  for  $n$  for simplicity and then we will substitute back at the end. Also, let us scale everything up by  $2,$  noting that this will have no effect on the ratio of  $\mathcal I$  to  $\mathcal U.$  We proceed with coordinate-bashing.

Let the vertices of  $\mathcal R$  be defined by the coordinates  $(-1,0), (0,n), (1,0), (0,-n)$  and therefore the vertices of  $\mathcal R'$  are defined by the coordinates  $(0,1), (n,0), (0,-1), (0,-n).$ Now, let us only pay attention to the first quadrant.

By symmetry, the intersection point of the two sides of  $\mathcal R$  and  $\mathcal R'$  in this quadrant will lie on the line  $y=x$  and it is not difficult to find that the equation of one of the sides is   $$ y=-\frac{1}{n}{x+1}, $$   so it follows that the intersection point is   $$ \left(\frac{n}{n+1}, \frac{n}{n+1}\right). $$  Therefore, the part of  $\mathcal I$  that lies in the first quadrant has vertices  $(0,0), (0,1), (\tfrac{n}{n+1}, \tfrac{n}{n+1}),$  and  $(1,0),$  so the Sholeace Formula tells us that the area of this quadrilateral is   $$ \frac{n}{n+1}. $$  The total area of  $\mathcal I$  is thus   $$ 4 \cdot \frac{n}{n+1}, $$   so the desired ratio is
\begin{align*}
\frac{[\mathcal I]}{[\mathcal U]} &= \frac{4 \cdot \tfrac{n}{n+1}}{\tfrac{1}{2} \cdot 2n \cdot 2 - 4 \cdot \tfrac{n}{n+1}} 
&= \frac{\tfrac{1}{n+1}}{1-\tfrac{1}{n+1}} 
&= \frac{1}{n}.
\end{align*}
Hence, substituting back, the answer is   $$ \boxed{\frac{1}{2023}}. $$   $\square$

