<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let  $[ABCD]$  denote the volume of tetrahedron  $ABCD$  and similarly for  $[AXYZ].$  Let  $s$  be the side length of  $ABCD.$ It is not difficult to see that   $$ \frac{[ABCD]}{[AXYZ]} = \frac{AB \cdot AC \cdot AD}{AX \cdot AY \cdot AZ}. $$  Using the fact that the volume of a regular tetrahedron with side length  $s$  is   $$ \frac{s^3}{6\sqrt{2}} $$   and   $$ AB=AC=AD=s, $$   it follows that   $$ [AXYZ] = \frac{AX \cdot AY \cdot AZ}{6\sqrt{2}}. $$  By Law of Cosines in  $\triangle{AXY},$  we get
\begin{align}
AX^2+AY^2-AX \cdot AY=49,
\end{align}
 and doing the same thing in  $\triangle{AYZ}$  yields   $$ AZ^2+AY^2-AZ \cdot AY = 49. $$  Subtracting these two equations, we get
\begin{align*}
AX^2-AZ^2+AY(AZ-AX)=0 &\implies (AX-AZ)(AX+AZ) + AY(AZ-AX)=0 
&\implies (AX+AZ-AY)(AX-AZ)=0.
\end{align*}
We are given that  $AX, AY,$  and  $AZ$  are all distinct, so it follows that  $AX \neq AZ$  which implies that   $$ AY = AX+AZ. $$  Now, substituting this into equation  $(1)$  yields
\begin{align*}
AX^2+(AX+AZ)^2-AX \cdot (AX+AZ) = 49 &\implies AX^2+AX^2+2 \cdot AX \cdot AZ + AZ^2 - AX^2 - AX \cdot AZ = 49 
&\implies AX^2 + AX \cdot AZ + AZ^2=49.
\end{align*}
Applying the Law of Cosines in  $\triangle{AXZ}$  gives
\begin{align}
AX^2-AX \cdot AZ + AZ^2 = 25.
\end{align}

Subtracting this equation from the previous equation then gives us   $$ 2 \cdot AX \cdot AZ = 24 \implies AX \cdot AZ = 12. $$  However, we can also manipulate equation  $(2)$  a little bit to get
\begin{align*}
25 &= AX^2 + AZ^2 - AX \cdot AZ 
&= (AX+AZ)^2 - 3 \cdot AX \cdot AZ 
&= AY^2 -3 \cdot 12 
&= AY^2 - 36,
\end{align*}
which means that   $$ AY^2 = 61 \implies AY = \sqrt{61}. $$  Hence, the answer is
\begin{align*}
\frac{AX \cdot AY \cdot  AZ}{6\sqrt{2}} &= \frac{(AX \cdot AZ) \cdot  AY}{6\sqrt{2}} 
&= \frac{12 \cdot \sqrt{61}}{6\sqrt{2}} 
&= \frac{12 \cdot \sqrt{122}}{12} 
&= \boxed{\sqrt{122}}.
\end{align*} $\square$</details>

