Note that it is just the sum of the coefficients of the odd powered  $x$  of the following polynomial:
\begin{align*}
    \prod_{n = 1}^{100} \left(1 - \frac1{2(n + 1)^2} + \frac{x}{2(n + 1)^2}\right)
\end{align*}
By using ROUF, at  $x = 1,$  we get 1, and  $x = -1$  gives  $\frac{51}{101}.$  Thus, the answer is  $\frac12\left(1 - \frac{51}{101}\right) = \boxed{\frac{25}{101}}$

Consider the generating function \[A(x) = \prod_{n=1}^{100} \dfrac{x}{2(n+1)^2}+\dfrac{2(n+1)^2-1}{2(n+1)^2}.\] We just need to find the sum of the coeffients of the terms with odd degree. Hence, 
\begin{align*}
A(1)-A(-1) &= \prod_{n=1}^{100} \dfrac{1}{2(n+1)^2}+\dfrac{2(n+1)^2-1}{2(n+1)^2} - \prod_{n=1}^{100} \dfrac{-1}{2(n+1)^2}+\dfrac{2(n+1)^2-1}{2(n+1)^2} 
&= 1 - \prod_{n=1}^{100} \dfrac{(n+1)^2-1}{(n+1)^2} 
&= 1 - \prod_{n=1}^{100} \dfrac{n}{n+1}\cdot\dfrac{n+2}{n+1} 
&= 1 - \dfrac{1}{101}\cdot\dfrac{102}{2} 
&= \dfrac{50}{101}
\end{align*}
which we divide by  $2$  to get an answer of  $\boxed{\dfrac{25}{101}}$  as required.  $\square$

