This seems to be the only one that hasn't been answered yet, so I'll post my solution. First, note that
  \[ \int_0^1 x^n\ln(x)\,dx = \left[\tfrac{1}{n+1} x^{n+1} \ln(x)\right]_0^1-\int_0^1 \tfrac{1}{n+1} x^n\,dx = -\tfrac{1}{(n+1)^2}\]
for all real  $n>0$ . Now, note that  $\ln(x^2)=2\ln(x)$  and use the Taylor expansion of  $\ln(1+x)$  to get
\begin{align*}
\int_0^1 x\ln(x^2)\ln(1+x)\, dx &= 2\int_0^1 \sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{(-1)^{n+1} x^{n+1} \ln(x)}{n}
&=2 \sum_{n=1}^\infty\int_0^1  \frac{(-1)^{n+1} x^{n+1} \ln(x)}{n}
&=\sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{2(-1)^n}{n(n+2)^2}
&=\sum_{n=1}^\infty \left[\frac{\frac{1}{2}(-1)^n}{n}-\frac{\frac{1}{2}(-1)^n}{n+2}-\frac{(-1)^n}{(n+2)^2}\right]
\end{align*}
The first two terms telescope to  $-\tfrac{1}{2}+\tfrac{1}{4}=-\tfrac{1}{4}$ , and the latter sum converges to
\begin{align*}
\sum_{i=3}^\infty \frac{(-1)^n}{n^2}&=\frac{3}{4}+\sum_{i=1}^\infty \frac{(-1)^n}{n^2}
&=\frac{3}{4}-\sum_{i=1}^\infty \frac{1}{n^2} +2\sum_{i=1}^\infty \frac{1}{(2n)^2}
&=\frac{3}{4}-\frac{\pi^2}{6}+2\cdot\frac{\pi^2}{24}=\frac{3}{4}-\frac{\pi^2}{12}
\end{align*}
Putting this together, we arrive at a final answer of  $-\frac{1}{4}-\left(\frac{3}{4}-\frac{\pi^2}{12}\right)=\boxed{\frac{\pi^2}{12}-1}$ .
